  "slug": "visual-activity-hierarchical-2c1b",
  "summary": "Four preprints use recurrent and normative models to explain serial dependence, thalamocortical history signals, replay dynamics and multisensory integration.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of recurrent and normative models to explain how past activity and noisy inputs shape current neural computation and behavior. Recurrent autoencoders trained to denoise videos reproduce human serial dependence in orientation judgments, framing it as a consequence of noise-robust optimization. A circuit model of thalamocortical PUL-PPC replay shows how history signals are reconstructed and redirect later choices. A second-order Langevin framework for noisy RNN hippocampal replay attributes exploration to adaptation and temporal compression to momentum. A review of normative and neural network models of multisensory integration supplies equations, Python code and a comparison of model assumptions. The items differ in scope; the review is a synthesis rather than a new result. What is new is the derivation of specific phenomena from explicit objectives or dynamics.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
